<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorieus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 510 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dorieus was a Spartan prince, a son of King Anaxandridas II and half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-i">Cleomenes I</a>. Passed over for the throne, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> around 515 BCE to found a colony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinyps">Cinyps</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, but was expelled by the Macae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>. He then sailed to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where he was killed fighting the Carthaginians and Segestans; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a> was his younger brother.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dorieus belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a>, and his story is known mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>. According to Herodotus, his father Anaxandridas II had married his own niece, and when the marriage stayed childless for a long time the Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">Ephors</a>, fearing that the line of Eurysthenes would die out, asked him to divorce her. He refused, and the ephors proposed a second wife, though bigamy had been unknown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. Soon both wives were pregnant: the second bore Cleomenes, and a few months later the first bore Dorieus, followed the next year by Leonidas and Cleombrotus, who may have been twins.</p><p>On Anaxandridas's death the council named Cleomenes king as the eldest son. Dorieus challenged the choice and was overruled, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> calls him the finest young man of his generation and says Cleomenes was close to madness. Dorieus then asked the council for a body of men to found a settlement elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> in a fit of temper, making the grave mistake of not consulting the Delphic Oracle. After he and his followers had been driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinyps">Cinyps</a> in Tripolitania, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and heard of land in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> said to belong by right to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heracleidae</a>, from whom his family claimed descent. This time he consulted the Oracle, was told the land was his for the taking, and sailed again with his followers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the way, war broke out between the cities of Croton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sybaris">Sybaris</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and at Croton's request Dorieus took part in its attack on Sybaris. He then founded a colony named Herakleia near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eryx">Eryx</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, but soon afterwards he and most of his followers were defeated and killed by the Egestaeans with Carthaginian support; the historians Bury and Meiggs date his death to c. 510 BCE. The survivors moved to the southern coast and took Herakleia Minoa.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> remarks that had Dorieus stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> under Cleomenes, who had no sons, he would in time have become king; instead the throne passed to Leonidas, whose deeds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a> made him a hero. Dorieus features prominently in the novel The Etruscan by Mika Waltari.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dorieus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 515 BCE, together with the Carthaginians, they expelled the Spartan prince <b>Dorieus</b>, who had tried to found a colony on the Cinyps.</blockquote><a href="/state/macae">Macae</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Heraclea Minoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-520" aria-label="Sparta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5112 828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dorieus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5562 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5562 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-520">What was here in 520 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
